 \subsection{Ablation Studies}
 \label{sec:ablations}
 
-\paragraph{Systematic Architectural Progression.}
-To quantify the individual and cumulative contributions of the proposed components, Table~\ref{tab:components} details the step-by-step ablation progression from the baseline to the final configuration.
+\paragraph{Historical Configuration Inventory.}
+Table~\ref{tab:components} now records source run identities alongside the historical configurations. The records change multiple factors, training budgets, and selection criteria. They document the experimental trajectory rather than a controlled additive decomposition of the final detector.
 
-\paragraph{1. Backbone Architecture (A0 $\to$ A1).}
-Replacing MobilePIXOR's residual blocks with a ConvNeXt-style backbone using $7 \times 7$ depthwise convolutions, inverted expansion $e=2.5$, SiLU, and LayerScale raised Moderate mAP from $41.40\%$ to $47.60\%$ (\textbf{+6.20\,pp Moderate mAP}), while standalone throughput nearly doubled from $90.57$ to \textbf{169.97\,FPS}. Large depthwise kernels broaden BEV receptive fields with limited computational cost.
+\paragraph{1. Historical Backbone Configurations (A0, A1).}
+The archived B0 and B1 results are $41.40\%$ and $47.60\%$ Moderate mAP. B0 was archived at 38 of 100 planned epochs and uses an epoch-30 checkpoint; B1 completed 100 epochs and uses a loss-selected epoch-13 checkpoint. The $6.20\,\text{pp}$ difference is descriptive and cannot be attributed solely to replacing residual blocks.
 
-\paragraph{2. BEV Input Representation (A1 $\to$ A2 $\to$ A3).}
+\paragraph{2. Historical BEV Representations (A1--A3).}
